# Mylonite CODEOWNERS
#
# This file is enforced. `main` is protected by a repository ruleset requiring
# code-owner review, so an entry here is a merge requirement rather than a
# suggestion. See GOVERNANCE.md.
#
# The catch-all comes first and everything after it narrows: in CODEOWNERS the
# LAST matching pattern wins, which is the opposite of .gitignore.

*                                   @Abidemialade

# --- the trust base ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below controls what the project's own checks do. A change here can
# disable the machinery that would catch the rest of a diff, so these are called
# out explicitly rather than left to the catch-all — the point is that the
# reason is written down where a contributor reads it, not that the owner
# differs today.
#
# When a second maintainer joins, these are the paths that should keep requiring
# the lead maintainer specifically while the catch-all widens.

# Decides what runs in CI, and with which token permissions.
/.github/workflows/                 @Abidemialade
/.github/actions/                   @Abidemialade
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @Abidemialade
/.github/dependabot.yml             @Abidemialade

# The composite action downstream users run in THEIR CI. A change here executes
# in other people's repositories.
/gate-action/                       @Abidemialade

# Decides which checks run on a contributor's machine.
/.pre-commit-config.yaml            @Abidemialade

# Declares entry points, so it decides what the plugin loader imports at runtime.
/pyproject.toml                     @Abidemialade

# The repository's own guards, and the tests that prove they can fail. Gutting
# the tests is as effective as gutting the guard.
/scripts/                           @Abidemialade
/tests/test_reference_target_guard.py @Abidemialade

# Decides which strings the secret scanner is allowed to ignore.
/.secrets.baseline                  @Abidemialade

# The deliberately-vulnerable target, where insecure code is expected and a real
# backdoor is therefore cheapest to disguise. Includes its own tests/ and
# pyproject.toml, which execute in CI and are outside the inertness guard's
# scope — this review is what covers them.
/reference_targets/                 @Abidemialade

# The responsible-use and dual-use rules themselves.
/SECURITY.md                        @Abidemialade
/GOVERNANCE.md                      @Abidemialade
